findings.detail:
IMPLEMENTATION FACTS. A Stripe PaymentIntent with capture_method:'manual' holds funds for only 7 days, or up to about 30 days with Extended Authorizations, which only eligible merchant categories get, at 0.08% plus IC+ pricing for others. So a deposit that sits for months must be a captured charge that you refund on cancellation. The balance is a separate charge at ship time on a card saved with setup_future_usage:'off_session' (or a SetupIntent), with a 3DS/SCA fallback email if the off-session charge is declined.
Under the FTC Mail, Internet, or Telephone Order Merchandise Rule (16 CFR 435), any paid preorder, including a partial payment:
(a) must have a 'reasonable basis' for its stated ship time;
(b) must ship within 30 days if no date is stated;
(c) must send a prompt delay notice with a revised date, or state that no date can be given;
(d) needs the buyer's affirmative consent for delays over 30 days, with free cancellation;
(e) must refund within 7 working days of cancellation.
COPY PATTERN next to the button: 'Reserve for $49. Fully refundable, one click, any time before your Detent ships. Refunds land within 7 working days (usually minutes).'

The FTC Consumer Reviews and Testimonials Rule took effect Oct 21, 2024. Civil penalties are $53,088 per violation (2025), and the first warning letters went to 10 companies on Dec 22, 2025. It prohibits:
- fake or AI-generated consumer reviews and testimonials;
- insider reviews without clear, unavoidable disclosure;
- incentives conditioned on positive sentiment;
- suppressing negative reviews;
- fake social indicators (bot followers or views).
So: (1) beta testers who got free units carry an inline 'Received a free unit' tag, not a footnote; (2) review incentives, if any, are sentiment-neutral; (3) publish all star ratings, including negatives, and respond to them (Baymard: 89% of sites don't); (4) counters (reservations, profiles, stars) come only from real data sources, with an 'as of' timestamp; (5) press quotes link to the source article.

The scene is static unless the dial moves, so rendering continuously only drains batteries and spins up laptop fans. Keep a dirty flag plus an 'active until settled' loop:

```ts
let needs = false;
const invalidate = () => {
  if (!needs) { needs = true; renderer.setAnimationLoop(tick); }
};
function tick(t) {
  const moving = physics.step(t);
  display.flush();
  renderer.render(scene, cam);
  root.style.setProperty('--dial', physics.value.toFixed(4));
  if (!moving && !display.animating) { needs = false; renderer.setAnimationLoop(null); }
}
```

Call invalidate() from pointer and key handlers, resize (a ResizeObserver on the stage), profile or colorway changes, and context restore.

PAUSE: stop the loop when an IntersectionObserver reports the stage is off-screen, and on `document.visibilityState === 'hidden'`.

INVARIANTS: the environment (PMREM) and the contact shadow never change because the knob only spins about Y, so bake them once.

PAGE REACTION: let the page respond to the dial through one registered custom property: `@property --dial { syntax: '<number>'; inherits: true; initial-value: 0 }`. Consume it only in transform, opacity or color (compositor-friendly), never in layout properties. Keep the canvas `alpha: true` (the WebGPURenderer default) so page gradients behind the knob are CSS, not extra 3D.

PHYSICS: substep at a fixed 1/240 s inside rAF (max 8 substeps) so behaviour is identical on 60, 120 and 144 Hz displays.

KNURL (verified render):
- A diamond (30°) knurl uses the height function h = min(tri(u·T + v·R), tri(u·T − v·R)), with tri(x) = |fract(x) − 0.5|·2.
- T = 120 teeth around a 36 mm knob gives about 0.9 mm pitch, the look of a real medium knurl. R = 8 rows over the side height.
- Generate the normal map at build time from that function (4.4 ms in Node). It measures 2.2 KB as a 1024×64 PNG, or 1.3 KB as a tileable 128×64 with `repeat.set(8, 1)`.
- Texture settings: `wrapS = RepeatWrapping`, mipmaps on, `anisotropy = Math.min(8, renderer.capabilities.getMaxAnisotropy())` (with WebGLRenderer). Mips prevent moiré at distance.

TSL VERSION (WebGPU path, zero bytes):

```js
const tri = x => fract(x).sub(0.5).abs().mul(2);
const U = uv().x.mul(120), V = uv().y.mul(8);
const h = min(tri(U.add(V)), tri(U.sub(V)));
const fade = smoothstep(0.6, 0.25, fwidth(U));
mat.normalNode = bumpMap(h.mul(fade), 0.5);
mat.roughnessNode = float(0.36).add(h.oneMinus().mul(0.06));
```

The fwidth fade is a frequency clamp: it removes aliasing that procedural bumps otherwise show when small on screen. bumpMap, fwidth and the other nodes are exported by three/tsl r186.

ENGRAVED TICKS: laser-etched anodize looks lighter and matte. Use a 1024² CanvasTexture with 60 ticks (every 5th longer and brighter) on a `RingGeometry` just above the base top, with `MeshStandardMaterial({ map, transparent: true, roughness: 0.8, metalness: 0, polygonOffset: true, polygonOffsetFactor: -1 })`. It rendered crisply at 1200×630. For real engraved depth, derive a normal map from the same canvas (Sobel). Add an index mark on the rotating ring.
